---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/analytics";
const title = "Shopify email analytics for weekly operating decisions";
const desc = "Create a consistent scorecard for campaigns and journeys, investigate changes, and assign the next action without confusing attribution with causation.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Which metrics belong in a weekly review?",
    "answer": "Use consistent audience, eligibility, delivery, order and customer-feedback measures. Add journey enrollment and exit reasons, then tie each investigation to an owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is attributed revenue always last-click revenue?",
    "answer": "No. Attribution depends on the selected model and window. Shopify documents multiple models; retain settings alongside comparisons."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does higher attributed revenue prove a flow worked?",
    "answer": "No. Credit allocation differs from causal lift. Investigate audience and data changes and use a suitable comparison when the decision needs incremental evidence."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do I need a new analytics platform?",
    "answer": "Only if a defined reporting gap justifies it. First ask whether existing reports and a reproducible finance join can answer the operating decision."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-6 text-xl">{desc}</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="card prose-shopify">

<h2>Start with a decision, then define the metric</h2>
<p>The operator needs to decide what to pause, repair, expand or investigate. Use delivery and eligibility checks before evaluating creative performance. A revenue drop caused by an empty audience cannot be repaired by changing a subject line. Separate campaign scorecards from triggered journeys because their entry populations and observation periods differ.</p>
<p>For a campaign, retain the intended audience, final eligible recipients, sends, delivery events, clicks, attributed orders and opt-outs. For a journey, add unique enrollments, messages per enrollment, completion and exit reasons. Keep the event definitions and source alongside each field; an accepted send is not evidence of inbox placement.</p>
<h2>Make a metric dictionary</h2>
<p>Define revenue per recipient with a stated numerator and denominator. Specify whether revenue includes taxes, shipping, cancellations and refunds, and whether recipients means unique people or message deliveries. Do not compare a campaign's unique-recipient measure with a journey's per-send measure under the same label.</p>
<p>Maintain separate timestamps for enrollment, send, order and reporting extraction. Set a store timezone and currency policy. Mark incomplete observation windows so a newly launched flow is not compared with one that has had time to receive delayed purchases. Record data latency rather than converting a missing value into zero.</p>
<h2>Keep attribution settings visible</h2>
<p>Shopify's <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/analyze-marketing/marketing-performance">marketing performance documentation</a> describes multiple attribution models and the default last non-direct click view. Attribution is not universally last click. Record the selected model, lookback window and channel rules beside exported results.</p>
<p>Two dashboards can disagree because they assign credit differently. Do not add their credited revenue together. For investigation, sample order IDs and compare timestamps, refund status, identities and touchpoints. A UTM match can help trace traffic; it does not prove an email caused the purchase. The <a href="/guides/revenue-attribution">revenue attribution guide</a> covers the order ledger and causal experiment separately.</p>
<h2>A weekly review that produces an owner</h2>
<ol>
<li>Check missing events, delayed imports and changed audience definitions. Name any data problem before interpreting trends.</li>
<li>Compare each campaign or journey with a relevant prior period using matching definitions. Note seasonality, sale activity, stockouts and audience composition.</li>
<li>Inspect delivery failures, opt-outs, complaints and customer replies. Avoid one universal alarm threshold; set escalation rules with the sender's current requirements and your own baseline.</li>
<li>Choose an action with an owner, reason, expected observation and review date. Preserve the prior configuration so the change can be explained or reversed.</li>
</ol>
<p>A proposed investigation might start with a replenishment journey whose order rate fell. Check whether active subscribers were newly included, the source stopped sending product identifiers, or the item became unavailable before testing timing. Those explanations call for different fixes. This is an investigative example, not a measured merchant result.</p>
<h2>Join finance inputs without double counting</h2>
<p>Agree with finance on a contribution definition. Use net sales and consistently allocated product, fulfillment, payment and messaging costs where available. If the starting sales figure already deducts discounts, do not subtract the same discount again. Mark estimated or missing costs and separate fixed platform fees from variable sends.</p>
<p>Report attributed contribution as an accounting view of credited orders, not incremental profit. A profitable order may have happened without the message. When a decision requires causal evidence, plan a customer-level comparison with assignment, exposure and uncertainty documented. A fixed small holdout or short calendar window is not automatically adequate.</p>
<h2>Cohorts should answer a specific question</h2>
<p>Compare first-time and repeat customers, acquisition sources or enrollment periods when the distinction changes an action. Use equal follow-up windows and inspect refunds. Do not label a young cohort low value simply because older cohorts have had longer to reorder. Distinguish subscription renewals from discretionary repeat purchases.</p>
<p>For a hypothetical launch newsletter, split reporting by existing customer status to understand audience mix. For an automation, inspect entry source and exit paths to discover whether a branch simply receives more qualified people. Branch conversion differences are descriptive until assignment and eligibility support a causal comparison.</p>
<h2>Evaluate reporting tools with a reproducible task</h2>
<p>Ask finalists from the <a href="/apps">app catalog</a>, including Sequenzy if it is on the shortlist, to reproduce one campaign and one flow report from the same permitted sample. Require metric definitions, export keys, timezone, refund handling, attribution controls and access roles. Demonstrate an operator correcting a report and handing it to finance. Do not infer warehouse export or holdout capability from a dashboard screenshot.</p>
<p>Native reports plus a maintained spreadsheet may answer the current decision; another platform is justified when an identified gap warrants its cost and upkeep. Retain a report version, extraction date and change log. Review the <a href="/guides/segmentation">segmentation guide</a> when audience membership changes, and <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">checkout recovery</a> when the metric problem comes from an actual journey exit.</p>

  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
